{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 36, :comments ["<b>And you shall make a plate of pure gold.</b> According to the opinion of our Sages, of blessed memory (Arachin 16a), who said that the צִּיץ atoned for brazenness of forehead, one must consider the matter: for it is written ״וְהָיָה עַל מֵצַח אַהֲרֹן וְנָשָׂא אַהֲרֹן אֶת עֲוֹן הַקֳּדָשִׁים אֲשֶׁר יַקְדִּישׁוּ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Exodus 28:38), and our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Menachot 25a): these are consecrated offerings that are brought in a state of impurity — so how can the midrash be against the plain sense of the verse? And according to what we wrote above, that the צִּיץ atones for licentiousness that is out in the open, as it is said (Jeremiah 3:3) ״וּמֵצַח אִשָּׁה זוֹנָה הָיָה לָךְ״ — and everywhere you find a boundary of immorality, there you find holiness, and licentiousness profanes the holiness, as will be explained below in the portion of Korach on the verse ״כִּי כָל הָעֵדָה כֻּלָּם קְדֹשִׁים״ etc. (Numbers 16:3), ״וַיִּשְׁמַע מֹשֶׁה״ (Numbers 16:4), where they suspected him concerning another man's wife — accordingly we are able to say that they found a place for this derash to say: just as the צִּיץ atones for unspecified consecrated offerings that became impure, so too does it atone for ״מֵצַח אִשָּׁה זוֹנָה״ — the holiness of a person. And therefore ״קֹדֶשׁ לַה׳״ was written upon it, and both of them are found in it: for the צִּיץ itself atones for the impurity of consecrated offerings, but the fact that it was placed on the forehead specifically is in order to atone also for ״מֵצַח אִשָּׁה זוֹנָה.״ And for this reason it is said ״וְשַׂמְתָּ אֹתוֹ עַל פְּתִיל תְּכֵלֶת״ (Exodus 28:37) — it resembles the thread of techelet of tzitzit, about which it is said (Numbers 15:39) ״וְלֹא תָתוּרוּ אַחֲרֵי לְבַבְכֶם וְאַחֲרֵי עֵינֵיכֶם אֲשֶׁר אַתֶּם זֹנִים אַחֲרֵיהֶם״ — see how it saves from licentiousness; and like the story of that man who hired a prostitute for four hundred zuz and was saved by means of the tzitzit (Menachot 44a). And even if our Sages, of blessed memory, spoke of brazenness of forehead in general, nonetheless there is also profanation of holiness in it, for all brazenness of face certainly does not originate in holiness, but comes from the side of impurity — after the chamber of its conception was defiled; and this matter is self-evident." "And according to this approach there are three things here that save from licentiousness. The first: the צִּיץ itself, upon which was written קֹדֶשׁ לַה׳. The second is the thread of techelet, which resembles the thread of techelet of tzitzit, about which it is said ״אֲשֶׁר אַתֶּם זֹנִים אַחֲרֵיהֶם״ (Numbers 15:39). The third is the מִצְנֶפֶת, which atones for arrogance, and our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Sotah 4b): anyone who is arrogant is as if he comes upon all the forbidden relations, etc., and they said further (there, 4b): anyone in whom there is arrogance will in the end stumble with another man's wife, etc. And the reason for this matter will be explained, with the help of YHWH, in the portion of Korach on the verse ״כִּי כָל הָעֵדָה כֻּלָּם קְדוֹשִׁים וּבְתוֹכָם ה׳ וּמַדּוּעַ תִּתְנַשְּׂאוּ עַל קְהַל ה׳״ (Numbers 16:3), ״וַיִּשְׁמַע מֹשֶׁה״ (Numbers 16:4), where they suspected him concerning another man's wife, etc.; see there. Therefore it is stated concerning the צִּיץ: ״וְשַׂמְתָּ אֹתוֹ עַל פְּתִיל תְּכֵלֶת וְהָיָה עַל הַמִּצְנָפֶת״ (Exodus 28:37). And with this what Rashi explained is also resolved — that we must fulfill three readings: thread upon the צִּיץ, and צִּיץ upon the thread, and thread upon the מִצְנֶפֶת — for we may explain that the word ״עַל״ does not indicate the place of its placement, but rather is an expression of addition, to say that each one adds upon the other, since the intention of all three is one. And accordingly there is ״עַל״ that indicates the place of placement, and there is ״עַל״ that indicates addition, and the discerning eye will choose which of them indicates placement according to the implication of the verses."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:24:18.696592321Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}